Verdict up front: the README catalog itself is mostly a link list with nothing net-new for us (UIs, IDE extensions, exotic use cases — irrelevant to the pilot). The two catalog entries that name-check our exact gaps (`openspec-spec-driven-superpowers` — "superpowers-style planning and readiness gates"; `SuperSpec` — "Superpowers execution discipline for traceable idea-to-TDD-verified-code") I could NOT clone/read — external GitHub repos, only one-line descriptions available. Flagging them as a follow-up lead, not a source I can cite mechanics from.

What IS inspectable: this repo dogfoods the actual OpenSpec CLI on itself (`.claude/skills/openspec-{explore,propose,apply-change,archive-change}/SKILL.md`, `openspec/` dir with live changes+specs). That's where the real signal is.

RANKED PATTERNS TO ADOPT

1. Machine-readable readiness-gate object (small effort)
   Cite: `openspec-propose`/`openspec-apply-change` SKILL.md — `openspec status --change X --json` returns `applyRequires` (artifact IDs needed before implementation) + per-artifact `status: done/pending/ready`; `openspec instructions <id> --json` returns dependencies/template/context/rules.
   Slots into: GO/NO-GO gate + complexity-once step.
   Why: our GO/NO-GO is currently an LLM self-judgment over a checklist. A small JSON gate file (e.g. `requires: [...]`, per-artifact done/pending) that CI also reads turns the gate into something mechanically checkable, not just prompted — directly closes the "weak-model robustness" gap since a weak model can't talk itself past a file CI greps.

2. "Stance not workflow" spec-intake mode for a non-technical operator (small effort)
   Cite: `openspec-explore` SKILL.md — "curious not prescriptive," "open threads not interrogation," never auto-capture (always *offer* to save an insight), and a lookup table "insight type → which artifact to update."
   Slots into: the 👤spec Q&A/brainstorming step, i.e. the one part of the pipeline Pete actually touches.
   Why: this is the single gap-matching pattern — a scripted Q&A reads as an interrogation to a non-coder. Adopt the "offer, never force" capture discipline + the insight-routing table in the brief-writing skill. Directly serves "cut friction Pete FEELS."

3. Living, accumulating capability spec merged on archive (medium effort)
   Cite: `openspec-archive-change` SKILL.md step 4 — diffs each change's delta spec against `openspec/specs/<capability>/spec.md`, prompts "sync now (recommended)."
   Slots into: finish step (post-merge) and feeds back into future spec Q&A (skip questions already answered by existing specs).
   Why: our brief is currently per-PR/ephemeral. A persistent `specs/` dir updated by each merged PR gives Pete (and reviewers) a durable plain-language "what the front-end does" doc, and lets the next brainstorming round skip settled ground — matters more here than usual because Pete will generate many small PRs.

4. Declarative schema swap for scaling rigor (medium effort, LOW CONFIDENCE — inferred from a one-liner, not read)
   Cite: catalog entry `openspec-schemas` (intent-driven-dev) — "custom workflow schemas including minimalist and event-driven templates," selected via `schema:` in config (confirmed this repo's own `openspec/config.yaml` has a top-level `schema: spec-driven` field, so the mechanism is real, just this specific extension's templates are unread).
   Slots into: complexity-once step.
   Why: replaces "LLM rates trivial..xhigh" with "config declares which artifact graph applies" — more deterministic/auditable. Worth a prototype, not a proven recipe — I could not verify this extension's actual schemas.

ALREADY COVERED (validates, doesn't add)
- Review gate between proposal/spec phases (`openspec-reviewed-workflow`) ≈ our spec-review step.
- PR-triggered CI lint gate (this repo's own `.github/workflows/lint.yml` + `ci-integration` spec) ≈ our CI teeth.
- Structured Requirement/Scenario (WHEN/THEN) spec format (this repo's `specs/*/spec.md`) validates the shape of our "signable brief," nothing to change.
- `openspec-playwright` (3-agent self-healing E2E) ≈ our execute+code-review+CI loop; one-liner only, nothing extractable.

NOT WORTH TAKING
- Rest of the README (Kanban dashboards, IDE/editor extensions, VitePress rendering, novel-writer/video exotic use cases, competitor list) — catalog surface area, irrelevant to a Laravel/Livewire front-end pilot.
- `openspec-schemas by kmhalvin` (QRSPI) — acronym undefined, no accessible content, can't responsibly recommend.
- Adopting the OpenSpec CLI itself as literal tooling (`openspec new/list/status/instructions`, `.openspec.yaml`) — would bolt a second orchestration layer onto a pipeline whose spine is already superpowers; the 4 patterns above are cheaper to reimplement directly in our own skills/CI than to inherit a whole parallel CLI+config system for.

