Mined ~/kramtime/paralife's process docs (CLAUDE.md, docs/README.md, docs/STYLE.md, docs/BENCHMARKS.md, docs/history/gsd-graduation.md, docs/notes/headless-feedback-loop-adr.md, .planning/phases/11-*, BACKLOG.md). Ranked additions below, all distinct from the grafts already listed as taken.

## Ranked additions

**1. The "reject fence" + frozen-corpus split (docs/history/gsd-graduation.md, docs/README.md).** Biggest finding, and it inverts the source's usual role: paralife's OWN heavy phase-artifact tree (CONTEXT/PLAN/SUMMARY/REVIEW/VERIFICATION/UAT/SECURITY/DISCUSSION-LOG per phase, under `.planning/`) is explicitly *retired* — frozen, read for facts only, never for structure. The graduation doc names why: "phase-locking," "fixed-depth ceremony," a solo-dev-plus-frontier-model doesn't need external orchestration. It keeps a **reject fence** — a two-column `Rejected | Why` table of everything deliberately left out — plus a **live-vs-frozen doc map** (docs/README.md: one table, which layer is injected-every-session vs on-demand vs archived). *Slots into:* the `/build` complexity-once step and general pipeline hygiene — write a reject fence for your own pipeline now, before Pete's usage generates pressure to bolt heavy stage-docs back on. *Why it helps:* directly warns against over-mining this source — the CONTEXT/PLAN/SUMMARY/.../SECURITY per-phase structure you're looking at in `.planning/` is exactly what its own author walked away from as ceremony that didn't earn its keep for a solo-or-small operator. Adopt the *habit* (a living CLAUDE.md + on-demand docs + a dated reject-fence table), not the retired artifact tree. *Effort:* S — one table, written once, appended to as decisions get made.

**2. Honest-measurement doc pattern (docs/BENCHMARKS.md).** Every claimed figure traces to a saved, fixed-name raw artifact (`docs/benchmarks/bench-*.json`, gitignored raw runs copied out under citable names); an explicit **gate command** applied to every report and shown inline (`jq -e '...'`); a **Caveats** section stating what wasn't proven (single unseeded sample, decaying-max metric semantics, "empty-category honesty" — a 0 that means "never exercised" not "passed"); and a closing line "no test assertion was added by this task" separating observation from gating. *Slots into:* the open measurement/eval-harness gap directly — whenever a build/PR includes a perf, load, or "does this actually work at scale" claim, produce one of these instead of a prose assertion. *Why it helps:* this is the single most concrete answer to "how do you let an LLM report a number honestly" — cite the raw file, name the gate, name what's NOT shown. Prevents an agent (or Pete) from being talked into "yes it's fast enough" without an artifact. *Effort:* M — needs a template + the discipline that every perf/eval claim gets a small companion doc, but no new tooling.

**3. Grep-verified doc-style rules (docs/STYLE.md, G1–G10).** Each editorial rule pairs with a literal shell command that verifies it held (e.g. G3: anchor set must be a superset pre/post trim, checked via `grep -oE ... | sort -u` diff; G6: banned review-process residue via regex; G9: keep/trim litmus as an eyeball fallback only when no grep applies). *Slots into:* code-review / spec-review loop convergence — this is a way to make "did the editorial pass actually preserve the contract" checkable rather than trusted. *Why it helps:* directly serves "review loops converge honestly" — instead of an agent asserting "I preserved all the important bits when I trimmed," it runs the diff-of-anchors command and shows zero loss. Cheap to adapt: for Pete's specs, pick 2-3 rules (e.g. "every acceptance criterion keeps its file:line cite," "no leftover review-round labels in the merged spec") each with a one-line grep. *Effort:* S — 2-3 rules, each a single grep/diff command.

**4. Compact decision-register table with a Status lifecycle (docs/history/gsd-graduation.md "Open questions" table: `# | Question | Status (dated)`, values Open/Resolved/Piloting).** Distinct from a full ADR — one row per decision, one dated status cell, updated in place as it resolves. *Slots into:* the plan/plan-review step, as the running decision log for anything not worth a full spec-doc. *Why it helps:* gives the pipeline a place to park "we decided X, revisit if Y" without spinning up a document per decision — and the dated status column means a stale "Open" is visible at a glance (convergence honesty: an item that's been Open for 10 phases is a smell). *Effort:* S — one markdown table in an existing living doc (BACKLOG.md-adjacent).

**5. Lightweight UAT artifact shape (.planning/phases/11-*/11-UAT.md).** Small frontmatter (status/phase/source/started/updated) + numbered test list (`expected:` / `result: pass|blocked|issue` / `blocked_by:` / `reason:`) + a tally summary (`total/passed/issues/pending/skipped/blocked`). Human-writable and machine-parseable. *Slots into:* the 👤UAT step in `/build` — right now that's a human checkpoint with no prescribed artifact; this gives Pete (non-technical) a form to fill that's still gate-checkable (tally must reconcile, `blocked` items need a `reason`). *Why it helps:* converts "Pete looked at it and said it's fine" into something with a paper trail an agent or reviewer can audit later, at near-zero authoring cost. *Effort:* XS — adopt the shape as-is, drop the frontmatter if too heavy.

**6. Two specific verification techniques worth folding into your existing close-out gates, not as new steps:** (a) **Anti-Patterns Found sweep** — a grep pass for TODO/FIXME/stub/placeholder across only the files the slice touched, reported even when empty ("none detected" is itself evidence, not silence). (b) **Data-Flow Trace** — for any "wired up" claim, one row per value proving it's *produced by real code and consumed*, not just instantiated (source: paralife's own `Requirements Coverage` table catches "artifact exists" false-positives this way). *Slots into:* evidence-bound-done (already-adopted gate) — these are the two checks that make "done" claims resistant to "looks plumbed but nothing flows." *Effort:* XS — a checklist line, no new artifact.

