## Ranked lift-able mechanisms (beyond what's already noted)

**1. Causal (not TTL) staleness check on evidence — `src/agents/git-pusher-template.js:93-103,164-189`**
`getQualityGateBlockingReasons()` doesn't just check "is this evidence recent" — it compares each quality-gate's `completedAt` timestamp against the specific `IMPLEMENTATION_READY` handoff message's timestamp (`completedAt < lastPush.timestamp` → reject as "completed before IMPLEMENTATION_READY"). Gates are matched by `id`+`scope` and only the *latest* matching evidence counts (`findLatestQualityGate`). This binds evidence to the exact artifact version it certifies, not just a time window — stronger than "run within last N minutes."
- **Slots into:** the CI always-on layer / blocking LLM-danger-review gate before merge.
- **Why it helps:** prevents a stale green check (from a prior push, or a re-run against an old SHA) from silently satisfying the gate for a new commit — a real GH Actions footgun.
- **Effort:** low-medium — you already have commit SHAs; add "evidence.completedAt > push timestamp for THIS sha" as an explicit assertion rather than relying on GH's own staleness handling alone.

**2. Deterministic pre-gate before spending LLM tokens — `scripts/quality-gate-runner.js`**
A plain `execSync` step runs a project-detected or user-declared command (`.zeroshot-quality` file → project-config → auto-pass) and only on success does the pipeline proceed to publish `QUALITY_GATE_PASSED`, which is what unblocks the (expensive) LLM validators. On failure it re-flags a heuristically-detected command as `heuristic-failed` so re-detection is retried next run — self-healing without a human editing config.
- **Slots into:** the CI layer, ahead of the blocking LLM-danger-review — run php-lint/phpunit deterministically first, only invoke the LLM review if that passes.
- **Why it helps:** cuts LLM cost/latency on the common case (a syntax error or failing test) and gives a clean fail-closed default (only auto-passes when *nothing* is configured, not on ambiguity).
- **Effort:** low — you likely already have this shape via CI job ordering; the transferable idea is the self-healing "heuristic-failed → retry LLM detection" state machine, not the gate-running itself.

**3. Independent claim verification against ground truth — `src/agent/pr-verification.js:534-643`**
The orchestrator never trusts an agent's self-reported "I created PR #123 and merged it." `verifyPullRequest()` extracts the claimed PR number/URL from the agent's own JSON output, then independently re-fetches via the platform CLI (`gh pr view` etc.) and hard-fails with `VERIFICATION FAILED: Agent claimed URL X, but CLI reports Y` on any mismatch (`validatePrUrl`, `validatePrClaims`). Same pattern polls for actual merge status rather than believing "merged: true" in the agent's output.
- **Slots into:** the `finish` step — before advancing past draft-PR→CI→CODEOWNERS→merge, independently query the GitHub API for PR state/CI conclusion/review state instead of trusting the agent's last message.
- **Why it helps:** closes the "agent hallucinated success" hole for a non-technical operator who can't eyeball-catch a fabricated "done" claim.
- **Effort:** medium — needs a small verification step wired after any agent-claimed completion, using `gh` directly.

**4. Context-replay allow-list — `src/agent/context-replay-policy.js`**
Ledger messages carry a `contextSafe`/`replayPolicy` flag. Default rule: everything is replayable into a downstream agent's context **except** raw `AGENT_OUTPUT` messages, unless explicitly marked `contextSafe:true`. So the full audit trail (ledger) is a superset of what actually gets fed back into subsequent agents' prompts.
- **Slots into:** the spec→plan→execute→review handoffs. Raw tool/file/web output an agent produced mid-task shouldn't automatically become "trusted context" for the next stage.
- **Why it helps:** narrows prompt-injection surface — a malicious/malformed string picked up while reading a file or fetching a URL in one stage can't silently ride into the next agent's system context just because it was logged.
- **Effort:** medium — requires your own message/handoff format to carry an explicit safe/unsafe tag; nothing to lift verbatim, but the allow-list-by-default-deny design is worth copying.

**5. Ledger token/cost rollup by pipeline role — `src/ledger.js:463-532` (`getTokensByRole`)**
Every `TOKEN_USAGE` message already in the event log gets aggregated per agent role (planner/implementer/validator) into input/output/cache tokens + cost, plus a `_total`. It's just a `SELECT` + reduce over the same SQLite ledger used for provenance — no separate telemetry system.
- **Slots into:** the open "measurement/eval harness" gap you flagged.
- **Why it helps:** gives per-stage cost/latency visibility for free once you have *any* structured event log — useful for knowing which stage (spec-review loop? code-review loop?) is burning the budget on a given branch.
- **Effort:** low if you already emit structured per-step events; medium if building the log from scratch.

**Lower-confidence 6th:** `src/command-proofs.js` lets spec/issue text embed a fenced ```` ```zeroshot-command-proofs ```` JSON block naming exact verification commands per id, merged (by id) with repo-level defaults, and converted directly into quality gates. Nice fit for OpenSpec brief → CI (spec Q&A stage emits the verification command per AC id, CI runs exactly that). Didn't dig deep enough to be fully confident on ergonomics; flagging as worth a closer look, not a firm recommendation.

## Anti-patterns to avoid (beyond auto-merge)

- **CANNOT_VALIDATE/SKIPPED whitelisted in the actual gating code, not just prompt text** — `git-pusher-template.js:256-273`, `hasSufficientEvidence()`: for each AC, `status === 'CANNOT_VALIDATE'` or `'SKIPPED'` → `return true` (non-blocking); only `CANNOT_VALIDATE_YET` blocks. This is baked into the JS trigger script that gates the merge, not a soft prompt suggestion — confirms your already-noted concern at the mechanism level. Make sure your red CODEOWNERS gate treats "skipped"/"cannot validate" as **not** green, full stop.

- **Validators are structurally forbidden from escalating to a human.** Every validator prompt in `full-workflow.json` ends with "No questions. Make a safe choice and proceed." Combined with the point above, a zeroshot validator has exactly one way to block a merge (`approved:false`) and zero ways to say "I don't know, ask a human" — `CANNOT_VALIDATE` doesn't block, and there's no escalate action at all. This directly collides with your pipeline's design ("Human fires on red-path OR complexity≥high OR LLM-escalate") — worth explicitly designing an escalate/CANNOT_VALIDATE-blocks path, since zeroshot shows what happens if you don't: ambiguity gets silently rubber-stamped.

- **Loop-breaking via a trust-the-metadata-flag convention** — `meta-coordinator` triggers in `full-workflow.json` check `message.metadata._republished` to avoid re-firing on its own republished messages. Works, but it's a single boolean with no integrity check; a dropped or forged flag either infinite-loops the coordinator or silently skips a stage. If you build anything with republish/relay semantics in your bus, don't copy this as-is — use a causal/id-based dedup instead of a mutable flag.

## Already covered (per your prompt)
Fail-closed handoff gates + staleness rule, AC JSON schema (id/criterion/verification/priority + PASS/FAIL/CANNOT_VALIDATE with command+exitCode evidence), blind validators (parallel, no cross-validator visibility until quality-gate), SQLite ledger as provenance substrate, docs-review spec-stage pipeline, git-pusher auto-merge, CANNOT_VALIDATE/SKIPPED-as-sufficient.

## Not worth taking
- Multi-provider adapter layer (`src/providers/*`, `capabilities.js`) — Claude/Codex/Gemini/OpenCode normalization is real engineering but irrelevant; your pilot is Claude-only (Desktop app + CI).
- `agent-stuck-detector.js` — /proc-based CPU/wchan/socket heuristics to decide if a detached headless process is "stuck." Their own comments admit high false-positive risk from single-indicator checks. You don't run detached long-lived agent processes; GH Actions timeouts + job status already cover this need far more simply. Don't import the complexity.
- `src/attach/*` (attach-client/server, ring-buffer, socket-discovery) — infra for reattaching to a detached multi-agent cluster's terminal. No analog in a worktree+PR pipeline driven from Desktop app + CI.
- Guidance mailbox (`ledger.js: queryGuidanceMailbox`) — lets a human inject mid-flight guidance to a running agent cluster without restart. Interesting in the abstract but Pete works turn-by-turn in the Desktop app, not against a live orchestrated cluster he'd message into asynchronously — no clear slot in your pipeline right now.

Files referenced (all under `~/kramtime/zeroshot`): `src/agents/git-pusher-template.js`, `scripts/quality-gate-runner.js`, `src/command-proofs.js`, `src/quality-gates.js`, `src/ledger.js`, `src/agent/context-replay-policy.js`, `src/agent/pr-verification.js`, `src/agent/agent-context-sources.js`, `cluster-templates/base-templates/full-workflow.json`, `src/agent/agent-stuck-detector.js`, `src/providers/capabilities.js`.


