/home/mark/.local/state/redact-transcripts/backups/c0a67591c07d3ccbc42e0171d8a84cb60f5cebb91ba4b09d3a362ce789bbb837.bak:729:{"parentUuid":"874a3fe7-a6bb-47ce-80ae-471785d13c59","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"4a508ad3-5ce5-4580-ae0b-257c42dc102b","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01P82gtUYfXxJn1M4bcKTU73","type":"tool_result","content":"# SessionStart Hooks and Plugin Hooks: Exact Mechanics

## 1. SessionStart Hook Configuration

### JSON Schema in Settings Files

SessionStart hooks are configured under the `hooks.SessionStart` key in settings files (`~/.claude/settings.json`, `.claude/settings.json`, or `.claude/settings.local.json`):

```json
{
  "hooks": {
    "SessionStart": [
      {
        "matcher": "startup",
        "hooks": [
          {
            "type": "command",
            "command": "/path/to/script.sh",
            "args": [],
            "timeout": 600
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}
```

### Matcher Values

Per the documentation:

> The matcher value corresponds to how the session was initiated:
> | Matcher   | When it fires                          |
> | :-------- | :------------------------------------- |
> | `startup` | New session                            |
> | `resume`  | `--resume`, `--continue`, or `/resume` |
> | `clear`   | `/clear`                               |
> | `compact` | Auto or manual compaction              |

Matchers are optional; omitting the matcher or using `"*"` fires on every session start regardless of source.

## 2. Plugin Hooks Location and Structure

### Where Plugin Hooks Live

Plugins ship hooks in `hooks/hooks.json` at the plugin's root. From the documentation:

> Define plugin hooks in `hooks/hooks.json` with an optional top-level `description` field. When a plugin is enabled, its hooks merge with your user and project hooks.

### Example Plugin hooks.json

```json
{
  "description": "Automatic code formatting",
  "hooks": {
    "PostToolUse": [
      {
        "matcher": "Write|Edit",
        "hooks": [
          {
            "type": "command",
            "command": "${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/scripts/format.sh",
            "args": [],
            "timeout": 30
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}
```

### Path Variables for Plugins

Three placeholder variables are available in plugin hooks:

| Variable               | Description                                                                                  |
| :--------------------- | :------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}` | The plugin's installation directory. Changes on each plugin update.                          |
| `${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_DATA}` | The plugin's persistent data directory for dependencies and state surviving plugin updates. |
| `${CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR}` | The project root.                                                                            |

From the documentation:

> Plugin hooks additionally substitute [`${user_config.*}`](/en/plugins-reference#user-configuration) values, in exec form only: the value is substituted into `command` and into each `args` element as a plain string, so no shell re-parses it.

## 3. SessionStart Context Injection: Exact JSON Structure

### Three Methods to Return Context

#### Method 1: Plain stdout (simplest)

Any text printed to stdout is automatically added as context for Claude:

```bash
#!/bin/bash
echo "Current branch: main
Uncommitted changes: src/auth.ts"
exit 0
```

From the documentation:

> Since plain stdout already reaches Claude for this event, a hook that only loads context can print to stdout directly without building JSON.

#### Method 2: JSON with hookSpecificOutput.additionalContext

Exit 0 and return JSON with `additionalContext` inside `hookSpecificOutput`:

```json
{
  "hookSpecificOutput": {
    "hookEventName": "SessionStart",
    "additionalContext": "Current branch: feat/auth-refactor\nUncommitted changes: src/auth.ts, src/login.tsx\nActive issue: #4211 Migrate to OAuth2"
  }
}
```

#### Method 3: Multiple SessionStart Fields

The complete set of fields available in `hookSpecificOutput` for SessionStart:

```json
{
  "hookSpecificOutput": {
    "hookEventName": "SessionStart",
    "additionalContext": "...",
    "sessionTitle": "auth-refactor",
    "initialUserMessage": "Help me fix the OAuth2 integration",
    "watchPaths": ["/absolute/path/to/file1", "/absolute/path/to/file2"],
    "reloadSkills": true
  }
}
```

### Field Semantics

| Field                | Description                                                                                                                                                                                                                                |
| :------------------- | :----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `additionalContext`  | String added to Claude's context at the start of the conversation, before the first prompt.                                                                                                                                                |
| `sessionTitle`       | Sets the session title with the same effect as `/rename`. Only applies when `source` is `"startup"` or `"resume"`.                                                                                                                        |
| `initialUserMessage` | String used as the first user message of the session. In non-interactive mode (`-p`), becomes the first turn even if no prompt is provided.                                                                                                |
| `watchPaths`         | Array of absolute paths to watch for [FileChanged](#filechanged) events during this session.                                                                                                                                               |
| `reloadSkills`       | Boolean. When `true`, Claude Code re-scans the skill and command directories after SessionStart hooks complete, making newly installed skills available in the same session.                                                                |

## 4. SessionStart Hook Input (stdin)

### JSON Passed on stdin

SessionStart hooks receive this JSON on stdin:

```json
{
  "session_id": "abc123",
  "transcript_path": "/Users/.../.claude/projects/.../00893aaf-19fa-41d2-8238-13269b9b3ca0.jsonl",
  "cwd": "/Users/...",
  "hook_event_name": "SessionStart",
  "source": "startup",
  "model": "claude-sonnet-5"
}
```

### Input Fields

| Field           | Description                                                                                                                                                                 |
| :-------------- | :-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `session_id`    | Current session identifier                                                                                                                                                  |
| `transcript_path` | Path to conversation JSON                                                                                                                                                 |
| `cwd`           | Current working directory when the hook is invoked                                                                                                                         |
| `hook_event_name` | Always `"SessionStart"`                                                                                                                                                    |
| `source`        | How the session started: `"startup"` for new, `"resume"` for resumed, `"clear"` after `/clear`, or `"compact"` after compaction. Allows matcher filtering.               |
| `model`         | The active model identifier. **Optional** — may be omitted after `/clear` or when a session is restored through conversation recovery.                                    |
| `agent_type`    | The agent name if launched with `claude --agent <name>`. **Optional**.                                                                                                    |
| `session_title` | Current session title if already set (e.g., via `--name` or `/rename`). **Optional**.                                                                                    |

### Environment Variables

From the documentation:

> Handlers run in the current directory with Claude Code's environment. The `$CLAUDE_CODE_REMOTE` environment variable is set to `"true"` in remote web environments and not set in the local CLI.

Additionally, path placeholders are exported as environment variables:

- `$CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR`
- `$CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT` (for plugin hooks)
